+# BLOCKED: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz does not match SHA256SUMS
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_release_artifacts_verify` (the CI check that runs `verify("SHA256SUMS", "artifacts")`)
+cannot pass: the artifact on disk is not the artifact the manifest describes.
+
+```
+SHA256SUMS:        7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD  libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+actual (sha256):   fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce  artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+```
+
+The other two artifacts match the manifest exactly (cli-2.0.1.zip and docs-2026.10.tar.gz).
+
+## Why it can't be fixed here
+
+* There is no source tree or build recipe for `libfoo` in this repository, so the artifact
+  cannot be rebuilt.
+* There is no second copy: `git log --all` has a single commit, and
+  `git cat-file --batch-all-objects` contains only the one 4000-byte blob for
+  `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`. Nothing else in the project contains the archive.
+
+The only "fixes" available are to recompute `SHA256SUMS` from the bytes currently on disk,
+or to relax/skip the check. Both would defeat the purpose of the release gate: they would
+bless whatever bytes happen to be present, when the mismatch may mean the artifact was
+corrupted in transit or replaced. That decision needs someone who knows which copy is
+authoritative.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Obtain the genuine `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` built/published for this release and place it in
+`artifacts/`, then re-run `python3 verify.py` and the tests. Its SHA-256 must equal the
+value in `SHA256SUMS` (`7BEE020A...356BD`). If, and only if, the release process
+legitimately changed the artifact, regenerate the manifest from the known-good artifact and
+re-publish both together.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`verify.py` had two real bugs that are now corrected:
+
+1. It opened artifacts in text mode (`open(path)`) and hashed `a.read().encode()`, which
+   raised `UnicodeDecodeError` on any binary artifact. It now reads in binary mode
+   (`open(path, "rb")`) and hashes the bytes directly.
+2. It compared the lowercase digest from `hashlib` against the manifest entry verbatim,
+   while the manifest stores uppercase hex. Hex digests are case-insensitive; the expected
+   value is now lowercased before comparison.
+
+After these fixes, `test_binary_files`, `test_reports_missing`, and the new behaviour of
+`python3 verify.py` are correct: the verifier reports `checksum mismatch: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`
+and exits 1, rather than crashing. Only `test_release_artifacts_verify` remains failing, for
+the data reason above.
